      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "By 1909 homesteaders began arriving, many coming by train to Inverness before heading out to find suitable land, with the largest influx between 1910 and 1912.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        16,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://centralmontana.com/communities/Inverness/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "After thriving in 1915–1916, extreme drought pushed many homesteaders away by 1918, leaving tar‑paper shacks to crumble while those who stayed built larger, more sustainable farms.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        19,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://centralmontana.com/communities/Inverness/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Amtrak’s Empire Builder runs through Inverness on BNSF tracks, yet the train never stops in this small Montana town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inverness%2C_Montana",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The historic Inverness Supper Club, built in the early 1900s, remains a landmark of the town’s early social life.",
